1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"build-the-diagram","number":"08","title":"Trace one flow before the full system.","mode":"Visual overload","scale":"slide","dek":"A diagram with many flows needs a starting point. Show who pays whom, follow the money back, and introduce the other accounts in stages.","before":[{"kind":"image","title":"Six-sector transaction diagram · PDF p. 46","image":"/originals/passarella-fmm-2022-p46.png","alt":"A Sankey diagram puts six paying sectors on the left, fifteen transaction categories in the middle, and six receiving sectors on the right. Gray bands cross repeatedly. Labels for small financial flows collide near the center and bottom.","id":"complete-transactions-diagram","sourcePage":"46"}],"after":[{"kind":"transaction-flows","stage":0,"title":"Household spending is revenue for firms.","revises":["complete-transactions-diagram"],"facts":[{"label":"Baseline","text":"t = 20 · nominal model units"}]},{"kind":"transaction-flows","stage":1,"title":"Firms pay wages and profits to households.","revises":["complete-transactions-diagram"],"facts":[{"label":"Baseline","text":"t = 20 · nominal model units"}]},{"kind":"transaction-flows","stage":2,"title":"Government receives taxes and buys output.","revises":["complete-transactions-diagram"],"facts":[{"label":"Baseline","text":"t = 20 · nominal model units"}]}],"diagnosis":"Six sectors appear on both sides of a Sankey diagram, with fifteen transaction categories between them. The gray bands cross repeatedly, and labels on the small flows collide. The audience has to discover both how to read a payment and which payment matters. The preceding equations do not provide a staged reading of this picture.","changes":["Begin with one payment: household consumption becomes firms’ revenue.","Keep the two sectors in place while adding wages and corporate profits flowing back.","Add taxes and government purchases; keep the complete financial and foreign accounts available as reference."],"preserved":"The five selected payment directions and amounts match the source’s baseline transaction matrix on PDF p. 19. The full six-sector diagram remains available in the original and linked deck.","deferred":"Trade, interest, investment, amortization, bank profits, and changes in financial positions. The three teaching views select five payment categories; they do not reproduce or close the full model.","practice":"Point to one payer, one payment, and its recipient. Add the next flow only after the audience can read the first one. Keep sector positions fixed as the picture grows.","principle":"Let the slide unfold","sourceType":"public","context":"Marco Veronese Passarella, 26th FMM Conference, October 21, 2022. The exported PDF has 77 pages, including incremental text and equation builds. PDF pp. 18–19 give the accounts; pp. 20–45 develop equations. The complete transaction diagram arrives once on p. 46, followed by a different, physical-flow diagram on p. 47. The critique concerns this diagram’s presentation.","beforeCaption":"Six sectors, fifteen transaction categories, and overlapping labels.","afterCaption":"Three builds: consumption, income, and government payments.","source":{"authors":"Marco Veronese Passarella","title":"Circular economy innovations in an input-output stock-flow consistent dynamic model","date":"October 21, 2022","url":"https://www.marcopassarella.it/wp-content/uploads/Presentation_1_FMM_2022.pdf","pages":"46; amounts on 19"},"values":["Source: 26th FMM Conference, October 21, 2022. Page references count physical PDF pages, including builds. The diagram is on p. 46; the nominal baseline transaction matrix is on p. 19.","The original already develops the model through equations on pp. 20–45. The local problem is the jump to one complete Sankey diagram: six sectors repeated as payers and recipients, fifteen middle categories, and colliding labels on the smallest flows.","Amounts transcribed from p. 19, baseline t = 20: households → firms, consumption 522.91; firms → households, wages 322.26 and corporate profits 383.80; households → government, taxes 142.97; government → firms, purchases 180.","These are nominal model units, not observed currency amounts or estimated effects. Arrow widths are schematic. The revised views track payment direction; they do not depict goods flowing in the same direction or establish a causal sequence.","Only five payment categories are shown. The foreign sector, banks, central bank, and the other transactions remain in the full source. The selected flows are not a balanced set of sector accounts, and government purchases are not assumed to equal tax receipts."],"topic":"Circular-economy modelling","point":"A dense flow diagram asks the audience to find its own route through the argument. Begin with one payer and one recipient, then add the return flows and the next sector while keeping the familiar objects in place. Here, that means consumption first, wages and profits next, and government payments after that. The complete accounts can remain available for reference; they need not be decoded all at once."}}]
